{"type":"underline","color":"green","pageRanges":[{"pageNumber":2,"rects":[{"leftRatio":0.5242,"topRatio":0.708,"widthRatio":0.3665,"heightRatio":0.0112},{"leftRatio":0.5047,"topRatio":0.7231,"widthRatio":0.3887,"heightRatio":0.0112},{"leftRatio":0.5047,"topRatio":0.7382,"widthRatio":0.386,"heightRatio":0.0112},{"leftRatio":0.5047,"topRatio":0.7511,"widthRatio":0.3855,"heightRatio":0.0134},{"leftRatio":0.5047,"topRatio":0.7684,"widthRatio":0.386,"heightRatio":0.0112},{"leftRatio":0.5047,"topRatio":0.7813,"widthRatio":0.386,"heightRatio":0.0134},{"leftRatio":0.5047,"topRatio":0.7986,"widthRatio":0.3889,"heightRatio":0.0112}],"text":""}],"comment":"In this section, we provide a trajectory going from a ResNet to a ConvNet that bears a resemblance to Transformers. We consider two model sizes in terms of FLOPs, one is the ResNet-50 / Swin-T regime with FLOPs around 4.5×109 and the other being ResNet-200 / Swin-B regime which has FLOPs around 15.0 × 109. For simplicity, we will present the results with the ResNet-50 / Swin-T complexity models.Tess","updatedAt":"2026-11-20T23:38:53.000Z"}
{"type":"highlight","color":"yellow","pageRanges":[{"pageNumber":3,"rects":[{"leftRatio":0.717,"topRatio":0.8589,"widthRatio":0.1764,"heightRatio":0.0114},{"leftRatio":0.5047,"topRatio":0.8741,"widthRatio":0.3887,"heightRatio":0.0112},{"leftRatio":0.5047,"topRatio":0.8892,"widthRatio":0.3866,"heightRatio":0.0112}],"text":""}],"comment":"Typically, the stem cell design is concerned with how the input images will be processed at the network’s beginning. Due to the redundancy inherent in natural images, a common stem cell will aggressively downsample the input images to an appropriate feature map size in both standard ConvNets and vision Transformers. The stem cell in standard ResNet contains a 7×7 convolution layer with stride 2, followed by a max pool, which results in a 4× downsampling of the input images. In vision Transformers, a more aggressive “patchify” strategy is used as the stem cell, which corresponds to a large kernel size (e.g. kernel size = 14 or 16) and non-overlapping convolution.","updatedAt":"2026-11-20T23:40:10.000Z"}
{"type":"highlight","color":"yellow","pageRanges":[{"pageNumber":4,"rects":[{"leftRatio":0.0819,"topRatio":0.095,"widthRatio":0.3076,"heightRatio":0.0112},{"leftRatio":0.3935,"topRatio":0.095,"widthRatio":0.0771,"heightRatio":0.0112},{"leftRatio":0.0819,"topRatio":0.1101,"widthRatio":0.386,"heightRatio":0.0112},{"leftRatio":0.0819,"topRatio":0.1251,"widthRatio":0.3889,"heightRatio":0.0112},{"leftRatio":0.0814,"topRatio":0.1393,"widthRatio":0.3078,"heightRatio":0.0122},{"leftRatio":0.3927,"topRatio":0.1393,"widthRatio":0.0753,"heightRatio":0.0122},{"leftRatio":0.0819,"topRatio":0.1553,"widthRatio":0.386,"heightRatio":0.0112},{"leftRatio":0.0819,"topRatio":0.1695,"widthRatio":0.3887,"heightRatio":0.0122},{"leftRatio":0.0819,"topRatio":0.1855,"widthRatio":0.386,"heightRatio":0.0112},{"leftRatio":0.0819,"topRatio":0.2006,"widthRatio":0.3889,"heightRatio":0.0112},{"leftRatio":0.0819,"topRatio":0.2157,"widthRatio":0.3889,"heightRatio":0.0112}],"text":""}],"comment":"Typically, the stem cell design is concerned with how the input images will be processed at the network’s beginning. Due to the redundancy inherent in natural images, a common stem cell will aggressively downsample the input images to an appropriate feature map size in both standard ConvNets and vision Transformers. The stem cell in standard ResNet contains a 7×7 convolution layer with stride 2, followed by a max pool, which results in a 4× downsampling of the input images. In vision Transformers, a more aggressive “patchify” strategy is used as the stem cell, which corresponds to a large kernel size (e.g. kernel size = 14 or 16) and non-overlapping convolution.","updatedAt":"2026-11-20T23:40:10.000Z"}"
`;

